+Thirty-second request (2026-09-29), "place-line": another bare-formula
+construction with no reference link — place a unit segment, place a second
+unit segment at a random angle touching an endpoint of the first somewhere
+along its own length, then grow indefinitely by having every later unit
+segment touch two untouched endpoints of two other segments, with no other
+intersections. Consistent with the standing literal-math pattern, but this
+one had a real interpretive fork worth recording: whether segments after the
+second keep the unit-length constraint (with a random "overhang" split
+beyond the two touch points) or are simply the literal straight connector
+between the two chosen points (whatever length results). Resolved it by
+proving the second reading is a structural dead end, not just less
+faithful: a literal connector's own two endpoints ARE the two points just
+consumed, so it creates zero fresh untouched endpoints and the whole
+construction can never continue past one such segment — incompatible with
+the request's own "every additional segment" phrasing implying an ongoing
+process. Built the unit-length reading, then discovered by running the exact
+(non-sampled) solver that even the correct reading jams almost immediately —
+median run ~7 segments, longest seen in the low-to-mid 20s across thousands
+of seeds. Treated that surprising result the way this requester has
+consistently rewarded surprising derived facts (Pentacell's mean-field
+presets, the domino/chess rule clarifications): stated it plainly, verified
+it wasn't a solver bug via a separate brute-force crossing check, and built
+the page around it — a live seed-sweep panel with a histogram — rather than
+cherry-picking one lucky long run to make the demo look more impressive than
+the math actually is. Worth reusing: when an exact/literal construction
+turns out to have a much smaller reachable state space than the request's
+phrasing implies, the honest move is a statistical view (sweep + histogram)
+of the real distribution, not a single best-looking instance.
